SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: where a step of a walked blend ends on a restriction, the
contacts' parameters on a periodic surface are moved by whole periods next
to those of the walk's point before (mitcad#2390).

The case: the rim between a loft's side and a cylinder pulled from its end
face, split at the points where the faces are tangent, each piece's
rounding vanishing in a point at both ends; the loft's side a periodic
surface (period 869.72 in u) whose face's seam lies on a convex piece's
way. That piece rounded with 2: the walk crossed the seam, the solver going
on into the next period (u 780.67 at its last point but one), and ended on
the restriction at the vanishing end (Blend_OnRst1), its contact on the
loft taken from the reframing at u -79.20, the same point a period below.
The approximation's start for the sections between the two points, the
chord between them in the surfaces' parameters, lay half a surface away;
the solver found balls 100 to 200 mm off, they were inserted into the line
as hard sections, and the approximation failed ("PerformSurf : Failed
approximation!"). With the walk's deflection 1e-4 the walk reached the
end on both arcs at once, the arc's curve giving u 790.52, and the piece
built.

- BRepBlend_Walking::InternalPerform: where a step ends on a restriction
  (either or both), the contacts' parameters on a periodic surface are
  moved by whole periods next to those of the walk's point before
  (RecadreIfPeriodic), after the classification of the reframing on the
  face, which wants them as the reframing gave them. The point in space
  is the same; where no period lies between, nothing changes.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2302.
